{"text": "GL Stele 212 vee ooey no.es2 Paid\n\nQuestions for Mr. Bible\n\nQuestions submitted by Rep. Gillmor\n\n1\n\n2\n\nIn the 1994 Inventory Buyout Agreement with the tobacco Ioan associations, Philip\nMorris, RJ. Reynolds, Brown & Williamson and Lorillard agreed to purchased 298\nrillion pounds of flue-cured tobacco that had been placed under loan by farmers.\nSubstantial discounts (up to 13% off the list price) were agreed to in return for guaranteed\npurchase intentions for the next seven years. Those purchase intentions were to be based\non market share of retail cigarette sales and meet or exceed 475 million pounds annually\nthrough the year 2000. In the fourth year of these contracted purchase intentions, the\nindustry submitted 454.6 million pounds in purchase intentions, in effect breaking the\nspirit of the contract with American tobacco farmers.\n\nPlease explain why the agreement to purchase 475 million pounds of flue-cured\ntobacco annually bas been broken.\n\nPlease explain your anticipated purchase intentions for all types of domestically\nproduced tobacco through the year 2003,\n\na\n\nv.\n\nRecent data reveals that imports of flue-cured tobacco has exceeded 200 million pounds\nannually, At the same time, 195 million pounds of domestically produced flue-cured\ntobacco failed to receive a minimum bid at auction and was placed under loan.\n\na, Please explain the cause for this sharp increase in imsported flue-cured tobacco.\n\nPlease explain whether the quality of imported tobacco is consistent with\n\nb.\ndomestically produced tobacco.\n\nPlease address whether imported tobacco meets U.S, standards regarding the use\nof pesticides and fertilizers,\n\nbLSozezoz", "words": [{"text": "GL", "x0": 221, "y0": 68, "x1": 240, "y1": 74, "conf": 12.0}, {"text": "Stele", "x0": 248, "y0": 68, "x1": 280, "y1": 75, "conf": 19.0}, {"text": "212", "x0": 287, "y0": 68, "x1": 306, "y1": 75, "conf": 45.0}, {"text": "vee", "x0": 314, "y0": 69, "x1": 333, "y1": 75, "c